<ref>Shibata, Joue. Saitama Sōsho. (1929) Volume 1: Chichibu Shi, p 111, Chichibu hikomikoto o keizu 『埼玉叢書. 第1巻』内『秩父志』内111頁「秩父彦命御系図」. Sanmeisha</ref> 
}}
Kasuga no Kami ([[:ja:春日神|春日神]]) also known as Kasuga Gongen ([[:ja:春日権現|春日権現]]) is a god of {{ill|Kasuga Taisha|en}}.
[[ja:春日神]]
[[zh:春日神]]
He has five components
{{ill|ja:春日権現|en}}
#{{ill|Takemikazuchi|en}}-o
# {{ill|Futsunushi|en}}
# {{ill|Ame no Koyane|en}}
# {{ill|Himegami|en}}
# {{ill|Ame-no-Oshikumone|ja|天押雲根命|en|draft:Ame-no-Oshikumone|simple|User:Immanuelle/Ame-no-Oshikumone}}


The four main kami enshrined at {{ill|Kasuga-taisha|en}} are {{ill|Ame-no-Koyane|lt=Ame no koyane|en}}, Himegami, {{ill|Futsunushi|lt=Futsunushi no mikoto|en}}, and {{ill|Takemikazuchi|lt=Takemikazuchi no mikoto|en}}.<ref>{{Cite book |author=Grapard, Allan G. |title=The protocol of the gods: a study of the Kasuga cult in Japanese history |date=1992 |publisher=University of California Press |isbn=0520070976 |oclc=25873140}}</ref> Though these are the primary divine beings of Kasuga taisha, they are often grouped together as a {{ill|Shinbutsu-shūgō|lt=syncretic|en}}, combined deity known as Kasuga {{ill|Myōjin|lt=Daimyōjin|en}}.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Tyler |first=Royall |title=The miracles of the Kasuga deity |date=2016 |publisher=Columbia University Press |isbn=9780231534765 |oclc=954193203}}</ref> Kasuga Daimyōjin is composed of five divine beings and each consists of a {{ill|Buddhism|Buddhist|en}} deity and Shinto kami counterpart. The fifth deity, {{ill|Ame-no-Oshikumone|en}}, was added much later and is said to be the divine child of {{ill|Ame-no-Koyane|lt=Ame no koyane|en}} and Himegami.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Ten Grotenhuis |first=Elizabeth |title=Japanese mandalas : representations of sacred geography |date=1999 |publisher=University of Hawai'i Press |isbn=0824820002 |oclc=39181008}}</ref> The importance of the multifaceted kami was that it became a template for future worshipers who wanted to combine several deities to pray to at once.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Ashkenazi |first=Michael |title=Handbook of Japanese mythology |date=2011 |publisher=ABC-CLIO |isbn=9781849728560 |oclc=755870995}}</ref>

== Content taken from {{ill|Himegami|en}} ==
{{ill|Hiraoka Shrine|en}} is located in the western foothills of the {{ill|Mount Ikoma|lt=Ikoma Mountains|en}} in central Osaka Prefecture. In its earliest days, it was a center for {{ill|mountain worship|en}} and the ''{{ill|kami|en}}'' of Kozudake, the peak immediately behind the shrine, came to be identified with {{ill|Ame-no-Koyane|en}}, the tutelary deity of the {{ill|Nakatomi clan|en}}, the ancestors of the {{ill|Fujiwara clan|en}}. There is no documentary evidence of when the shrine was first constructed, but it is believed to be sometime during the {{ill|Kofun period|en}}.  When the Fujiwara built {{ill|Kasuga Taisha|en}} in {{ill|Nara (city)|lt=Nara|en}}, two of the four ''kami'' enshrined  (Ame-no-Koyomi and Himegami) were ''{{ill|bunrei|en}}'' transferred from this shrine, and the {{ill|Hiraoka Shrine|en}} was thus given the name of ''Moto-Kasuga'' ("former Kasuga").<ref>{{cite book |last1=Yoshiki |first1=Emi |title=Zenkoku 'Ichinomiya' tettei gaido |date=2007 |publisher=PHP Institute |isbn=978-4569669304 |language=Japanese}}</ref><ref>{{cite book |last1=Okada |first1=Shoji |title=Taiyō no chizuchō 24 zenkoku 'Ichinomiya' meguri |date=2014 |publisher=Heibonsha |isbn=978-4582945614 |language=Japanese}}</ref>


The four main {{ill|kami|en}} each have a shrine devoted to them which are all in the same {{ill|Kasuga-zukuri|lt=architectural style|en}}. They are characterized by sloping gabled roofs, a rectangular structure, {{ill|katsuogi|en}} (decorative logs), and {{ill|Chigi (architecture)|lt=chigi|en}} (forked roof structures).<ref>{{Cite book |last=Cali |first=Joseph |title=Shinto shrines: a guide to the sacred sites of Japan's ancient religion |date=2013 |publisher=University of Hawai'i Press |isbn=9780824837136 |oclc=1043096844}}</ref> The first hall established is dedicated to {{ill|Takemikazuchi|lt=Takemikazuchi no mikoto|en}}, the second to {{ill|Futsunushi|lt=Futsunushi no mikoto|en}}, the third to {{ill|Ame-no-Koyane|lt=Amenokoyane no mikoto|en}}, and the final hall is attributed to the consort, Himegami.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Main Sanctuary |url=http://www.kasugataisha.or.jp/guidance/map3/k_map01.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190624041142/http://www.kasugataisha.or.jp/guidance/map3/k_map01.html |archive-date=2019-06-24 |access-date=2019-03-28 |website=www.kasugataisha.or.jp}}</ref>

