Kasuga no Kami ([[:ja:春日神|春日神]]) also known as Kasuga Gongen ([[:ja:春日権現|春日権現]]) is a god of {{ill|Kasuga Taisha|en}}.
[[ja:春日神]]
[[zh:春日神]]
He has five components
{{ill|ja:春日権現|en}}
#{{ill|Takemikazuchi|en|lt=Takemikazuchi-o}}
# {{ill|Futsunushi|en}}
# {{ill|Ame no Koyane|en}}
# {{ill|Himegami|en}}
# {{ill|Ame-no-Oshikumone|ja|天押雲根命|en|draft:Ame-no-Oshikumone|simple|User:Immanuelle/Ame-no-Oshikumone}}


The four main kami enshrined at {{ill|Kasuga-taisha|en}} are {{ill|Ame-no-Koyane|lt=Ame no koyane|en}}, Himegami, {{ill|Futsunushi|lt=Futsunushi no mikoto|en}}, and {{ill|Takemikazuchi|lt=Takemikazuchi no mikoto|en}}.<ref>{{Cite book |author=Grapard, Allan G. |title=The protocol of the gods: a study of the Kasuga cult in Japanese history |date=1992 |publisher=University of California Press |isbn=0520070976 |oclc=25873140}}</ref> Though these are the primary divine beings of Kasuga taisha, they are often grouped together as a {{ill|Shinbutsu-shūgō|lt=syncretic|en}}, combined deity known as Kasuga {{ill|Myōjin|lt=Daimyōjin|en}}.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Tyler |first=Royall |title=The miracles of the Kasuga deity |date=2016 |publisher=Columbia University Press |isbn=9780231534765 |oclc=954193203}}</ref> Kasuga Daimyōjin is composed of five divine beings and each consists of a {{ill|Buddhism|Buddhist|en}} deity and Shinto kami counterpart. The fifth deity, {{ill|Ame-no-Oshikumone|en}}, was added much later and is said to be the divine child of {{ill|Ame-no-Koyane|lt=Ame no koyane|en}} and Himegami.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Ten Grotenhuis |first=Elizabeth |title=Japanese mandalas : representations of sacred geography |date=1999 |publisher=University of Hawai'i Press |isbn=0824820002 |oclc=39181008}}</ref> The importance of the multifaceted kami was that it became a template for future worshipers who wanted to combine several deities to pray to at once.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Ashkenazi |first=Michael |title=Handbook of Japanese mythology |date=2011 |publisher=ABC-CLIO |isbn=9781849728560 |oclc=755870995}}</ref>

The four main {{ill|kami|en}} (excluding {{ill|Ame-no-Oshikumone|ja|天押雲根命|en|draft:Ame-no-Oshikumone|simple|User:Immanuelle/Ame-no-Oshikumone}}) each have a shrine devoted to them which are all in the same {{ill|Kasuga-zukuri|lt=architectural style|en}}. They are characterized by sloping gabled roofs, a rectangular structure, {{ill|katsuogi|en}} (decorative logs), and {{ill|Chigi (architecture)|lt=chigi|en}} (forked roof structures).<ref>{{Cite book|title=Shinto shrines: a guide to the sacred sites of Japan's ancient religion|last=Cali|first=Joseph|date=2013|publisher=University of Hawai'i Press|isbn=9780824837136|oclc=1043096844}}</ref> The first hall established is dedicated to {{ill|Takemikazuchi|lt=Takemikazuchi no mikoto|en}}, the second to {{ill|Futsunushi|lt=Futsunushi no mikoto|en}}, the third to {{ill|Ame-no-Koyane|lt=Amenokoyane no mikoto|en}}, and the final hall is attributed to the consort, {{ill|Himegami|en}}.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Main Sanctuary|url=http://www.kasugataisha.or.jp/guidance/map3/k_map01.html|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190624041142/http://www.kasugataisha.or.jp/guidance/map3/k_map01.html|archive-date=2019-06-24|access-date=2019-03-28|website=www.kasugataisha.or.jp}}</ref> Wakamiya Shrine is dedicated to the fifth new member {{ill|Ame-no-Oshikumone|en|Draft:Ame-no-Oshikumone|ja|天押雲根命}}.<ref>https://www.mlit.go.jp/tagengo-db/common/001559851.pdf</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Wakamiya Shrine {{!}} Search Details|url=https://www.mlit.go.jp/tagengo-db/en/H30-00882.html|access-date=2024-03-20|website=Japan Tourism Agency,Japan Tourism Agency|language=en}}</ref>

The two kami {{ill|Ame no Koyane|en}} and {{ill|Himegami|en}}  were ''{{ill|bunrei|en}}'' transferred from {{ill|Hiraoka Shrine|en}}, and the shrine was thus given the name of ''Moto-Kasuga'' ("former Kasuga").<ref name="Yoshiki-2007" /><ref name="Okada-2014" />

{{ill|Hiraoka Shrine|en}} is located in the western foothills of the {{ill|Mount Ikoma|lt=Ikoma Mountains|en}} in central Osaka Prefecture. In its earliest days, it was a center for {{ill|mountain worship|en}} and the ''{{ill|kami|en}}'' of Kozudake, the peak immediately behind the shrine, came to be identified with {{ill|Ame-no-Koyane|en}}, the tutelary deity of the {{ill|Nakatomi clan|en}}, the ancestors of the {{ill|Fujiwara clan|en}}.<ref name="Yoshiki-2007">{{cite book |last1=Yoshiki |first1=Emi |title=Zenkoku 'Ichinomiya' tettei gaido |date=2007 |publisher=PHP Institute |isbn=978-4569669304 |language=Japanese}}</ref><ref name="Okada-2014">{{cite book |last1=Okada |first1=Shoji |title=Taiyō no chizuchō 24 zenkoku 'Ichinomiya' meguri |date=2014 |publisher=Heibonsha |isbn=978-4582945614 |language=Japanese}}</ref>
