The four main {{ill|kami|en}} (excluding {{ill|Ame-no-Oshikumone|ja|天押雲根命|en|draft:Ame-no-Oshikumone|simple|User:Immanuelle/Ame-no-Oshikumone}}) each have a shrine devoted to them which are all in the same {{ill|Kasuga-zukuri|lt=architectural style|en}}. They are characterized by sloping gabled roofs, a rectangular structure, {{ill|katsuogi|en}} (decorative logs), and {{ill|Chigi (architecture)|lt=chigi|en}} (forked roof structures).<ref>{{Cite book|title=Shinto shrines: a guide to the sacred sites of Japan's ancient religion|last=Cali|first=Joseph|date=2013|publisher=University of Hawai'i Press|isbn=9780824837136|oclc=1043096844}}</ref> The first hall established is dedicated to {{ill|Takemikazuchi|lt=Takemikazuchi no mikoto|en}}, the second to {{ill|Futsunushi|lt=Futsunushi no mikoto|en}}, the third to {{ill|Ame-no-Koyane|lt=Amenokoyane no mikoto|en}}, and the final hall is attributed to the consort, {{ill|Himegami|en|4=simple|5=User:Immanuelle/Himegami}}.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|title=Main Sanctuary|url=http://www.kasugataisha.or.jp/guidance/map3/k_map01.html|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190624041142/http://www.kasugataisha.or.jp/guidance/map3/k_map01.html|archive-date=2019-06-24|access-date=2019-03-28|website=www.kasugataisha.or.jp}}</ref> Wakamiya Shrine is dedicated to the fifth new member {{ill|Ame-no-Oshikumone|en|Draft:Ame-no-Oshikumone|ja|天押雲根命|simple|User:Immanuelle/Ame-no-Oshikumone}}.<ref name=":1">https://www.mlit.go.jp/tagengo-db/common/001559851.pdf</ref><ref name=":2">{{Cite web|title=Wakamiya Shrine {{!}} Search Details|url=https://www.mlit.go.jp/tagengo-db/en/H30-00882.html|access-date=2024-03-20|website=Japan Tourism Agency,Japan Tourism Agency|language=en}}</ref>

The two kami {{ill|Ame no Koyane|en}} and {{ill|Himegami|en}}  were ''{{ill|bunrei|en}}'' transferred from {{ill|Hiraoka Shrine|en}}, and the shrine was thus given the name of ''Moto-Kasuga'' ("former Kasuga").<ref name="Yoshiki-2007">{{cite book|title=Zenkoku 'Ichinomiya' tettei gaido|last1=Yoshiki|first1=Emi|date=2007|publisher=PHP Institute|isbn=978-4569669304|language=Japanese}}</ref><ref name="Okada-2014">{{cite book|title=Taiyō no chizuchō 24 zenkoku 'Ichinomiya' meguri|last1=Okada|first1=Shoji|date=2014|publisher=Heibonsha|isbn=978-4582945614|language=Japanese}}</ref> Those two deities and their son {{ill|Ame-no-Oshikumone|ja|天押雲根命|en|draft:Ame-no-Oshikumone|simple|User:Immanuelle/Ame-no-Oshikumone}}<ref name="Ten Grotenhuis-1999">{{Cite book|title=Japanese mandalas : representations of sacred geography|last=Ten Grotenhuis|first=Elizabeth|date=1999|publisher=University of Hawai'i Press|isbn=0824820002|oclc=39181008}}</ref> are ancestors of the {{ill|Nakatomi clan|en}} and {{ill|Fujiwara clan|en}}.<ref>Shibata, Joue. Saitama Sōsho. (1929) Volume 1: Chichibu Shi, p 111, Chichibu hikomikoto o keizu 『埼玉叢書. 第1巻』内『秩父志』内111頁「秩父彦命御系図」. Sanmeisha</ref><ref name="Ten Grotenhuis-19992">{{Cite book|title=Japanese mandalas : representations of sacred geography|last=Ten Grotenhuis|first=Elizabeth|date=1999|publisher=University of Hawai'i Press|isbn=0824820002|oclc=39181008}}</ref>

{{ill|Hiraoka Shrine|en}} is located in the western foothills of the {{ill|Mount Ikoma|lt=Ikoma Mountains|en}} in central Osaka Prefecture. In its earliest days, it was a center for [[mountain worship|mountain worship]] and the ''[[kami|kami]]'' of Kozudake, the peak immediately behind the shrine, came to be identified with {{ill|Ame-no-Koyane|en}}, the tutelary deity of the {{ill|Nakatomi clan|en}}, the ancestors of the {{ill|Fujiwara clan|en}}.<ref name="Yoshiki-2007" /><ref name="Okada-2014" />
[[ja:春日神]]
