== History ==
'''Kasuga Gongen''' (春日権現) is a {{ill|syncretic deity|ja|神仏習合|1=Shinbutsu-shūgō}} in Japanese religion, associated with {{ill|Amoghapasha|ja|不空羂索観音|fr|Amoghapasha Lokeshvara |id|Arca Amoghapasa|jv|Arca Amoghapasa|zh|不空羂索觀音|simple|User:Immanuelle/Amoghapasha }}, {{ill|Bhaisajyaguru|en|4=fr}}, {{ill|Kṣitigarbha|en|4=fr}}, and {{ill|Ekādaśamukha|ja|十一面観音}} as its {{ill|honji-butsu|ja|本地仏}}. Kasuga Gongen is also referred to as "Kasuga Daimyōjin" (春日大明神). Before the {{ill|Shinbutsu bunri|ja|神仏分離}} and {{ill|Haibutsu kishaku|ja|廃仏毀釈}} policies during the Meiji Restoration, Kasuga Gongen was enshrined at sites such as {{ill|Kasuga Shrine|ja|春日大社|1=Kasuga-taisha}}.

Kasuga Shrine enshrines four deities: {{ill|Takemikazuchi|ja|タケミカヅチ}}, {{ill|Futsunushi|ja|経津主神}}, {{ill|Ame-no-Koyane|ja|天児屋命}}, and {{ill|Himegami|ja|比売神}}, with Ame-no-Koyane being the ancestral deity of the {{ill|Fujiwara clan|ja|藤原氏}} (specifically the {{ill|Nakatomi clan|ja|中臣氏}}). As {{ill|shinbutsu shūgō|ja|神仏習合|1=Shinbutsu-shūgō}} progressed, and as the capital moved from {{ill|Nagaoka-kyō|ja|長岡京}} to {{ill|Heian-kyō|ja|平安京}}, the political influence of the Fujiwara clan shifted from {{ill|Nara|ja|奈良}} to {{ill|Kyoto|ja|京都}}. During this time, {{ill|Kōfuku-ji|ja|興福寺}}, the Fujiwara clan's family temple, became the {{ill|jingū-ji|ja|神宮寺}} (guardian temple) of Kasuga Shrine and exerted significant control over it, effectively incorporating it into the temple's estate.

Starting in 1018 (Kan'nin 2), Kōfuku-ji priests began holding {{ill|Lotus Eight Lectures|ja|法華八講}} at Kasuga Shrine, increasing the site’s Buddhist influence. According to traditions, the deities of {{ill|Kashima Shrine|ja|鹿島大明神|Kashima Daimyōjin=Kashima Daimyōjin|lt=Kashima Daimyōjin}} (Takemikazuchi) and {{ill|Katori Shrine|ja|香取大明神|lt=Katori Daimyōjin}} (Futsunushi) were said to have been transferred to {{ill|Mount Mikasa|ja|御蓋山}} (Mount Wakakusa) for the protection of Buddhist law and state pacification.<ref>Kasuga Daimyōjin-ji</ref> Consequently, the deities were venerated as {{ill|Gongen|ja|権現}} under the {{ill|honji suijaku|ja|本地垂迹}} framework.

However, the monk {{ill|Jōkei (monk)|ja|貞慶|lt=Jōkei}} regarded the deity in the First Hall as {{ill|Śākyamuni|ja|釈迦如来}}.

The {{ill|Kasuga Gongen Genki E|ja|春日権現験記}} stated that those studying {{ill|Yogācāra|ja|唯識論}} in the {{ill|East Asian Yogācāra|ja|法相宗|lt=Hossō sect}} were protected by Kasuga Gongen. The creation of {{ill|Kasuga Mandalas|ja|春日曼荼羅}} also flourished, depicting the honji-butsu of the main halls (First to Fourth Halls) and subsidiary shrines like Wakamiya.

